findings.description:
`cast_vote_v0::handler` accumulates vote weight across multiple invocations by adding into `voter_weight_record.voter_weight` when `weight_action_target`/`weight_action` match, but it does not re-check that NFTs/positions previously counted are still controlled by `args.owner` at the moment the final `spl-gov::CastVote` is executed.

If the NFTs/positions used for voting are transferable (or can be temporarily acquired), an attacker can:
1) call `CastVoteV0` with NFTs they temporarily control, creating `NftVoteRecord`s and increasing the persisted voter weight;
2) transfer/sell/return those NFTs;
3) later call `CastVoteV0` again (with any remaining NFTs) and bundle the final call with `spl-gov::CastVote`, causing the final `VoterWeightRecord` to include weight from NFTs no longer owned.

This is a lifetime/control failure because the program treats “used once in the past” as equivalent to “owned now for this vote”.
